zach from the try guys has come out and  said that he has an autoimmune condition  i'm about to watch the video and react  to it let's get to it  i have had really bad back issues  basically my entire life perfect i'm an  osteopathic physician this is gonna go  really well i was going to the  chiropractor when i was 10. i had to  have like an ergonomic backpack in  middle school i mean everyone should  have an ergonomic backpack in school in  fact my theory is that a lot of the  scoliosis that kids are developing these  days come from uneven backpacks wearing  them on one shoulder or even those desks  where you can only lean on one side i  ache i get stiff it kind of started uh  before i went to buzzfeed a lot of my  time would be spent at a computer and i  noticed i would just  when i sneezed i could feel the pain  shoot from my back through the front  through my chest cage the way that he's  describing his pain right away tells me  that there's some kind of inflammation  happening at the rib joints because  remember the ribs attach to the front of  your chest the sternum at least the  upper ribs here and then also to your  spine in the back the thoracic spine i  have a bit of a hypochondria  and so whenever something's wrong with  me i'm like cancer it's cancer cancer so  i told my doctor about this pain he says  that's not a thing you're good if you're  a doctor and a patient's coming in with  a high level of anxiety about a  condition and you know they're  susceptible to health related anxiety  you have to right away be ultra  transparent and honest about what you're  doing and why you're doing it not just  give statements like you're good and  move on i decided it was time to go to  the chiropractor we took an x-ray turns  out i have scoliosis so scoliosis  doesn't always require treatment we  blame a lot of problems on patient  scoliosis when it's actually not the  main cause it's very easy in a patient  who's having back pain caused by  something completely different to get an  x-ray and blame it all on the scoliosis  in fact it's the lazy thing to do a  small degree of scoliosis shouldn't be  causing significant discomfort in fact  most cases of scoliosis that we see are  extremely mild and are asymptomatic now  for anyone who has scoliosis they will  be the first people to tell you that my  scoliosis is minor it's not because you  would get surgery for i tried a lot of  things to help this i changed my diet i  don't know what the diet would help with  scoliosis with unless he had a bone  mineral deficiency there the main thing  that you do for mild scoliosis like this  is not even bracing not surgery  obviously physical therapy doing some  exercise some lifestyle modifications  make sure you're not wearing a book pad  got only on one side the pain built to a  point where i couldn't sleep i woke up  at like 4 45 in pain this was every  night for for months  laying down  hurt  uh being in my bed  hurt  i mean living  hurt i'm talking about i would wake up  in the middle of the night five times  all during this i am i'm working a  full-time job but when you're not  sleeping it it eats away at you mentally  it absolutely does because you don't  recover well you're constantly in a  hyper sympathetic state where it's like  you're basically being chased by a lion  you're never relaxing you're never  digesting you're never repairing and  healing and as a doctor you have to ask  these things because when we ask how  much disability a certain injury is  causing you it's not about whether or  not you can walk or run or lift or not  it's also about can you rest adequately  can you sleep well can you sit well can  you perform the duties at your job well  i start screaming i think and i feel bad  for the doctor but i was like hey this  can't continue i'm losing my mind i got  referred to a rheumatologist and within  minutes the rheumatologist is like oh  yeah i think i know what's wrong with  you and i'm like what i'm interested  what conversation they had in the  medical office that led him to go to see  a rheumatologist who's essentially an  autoimmune joint disease expert why they  went that route versus a spinal surgeon  for example it could have been that he  wasn't interested in spine surgery but  there has to have been tip offs to some  sort of autoimmune process i had to go  in for an mri which was scary because at  that point i didn't know what was wrong  with me he just referred me to an mri  and i'm like okay oh god what is this an  mri is magnetic resonance imaging where  you have giant magnets essentially spin  around you to give us really good  quality images it's really loud it's  very claustrophobic some of my patients  actually need benzodiazepine medications  which are anti-anxiety medications to  even be able to get an mri they do a  great job at showing soft tissue like an  x-ray for example will show bone quite  well or joints well the mri would show  us cartilage tendons ligaments organs it  turns out i have ankylosing spondylitis  ankylosing spondylitis is basically an  inflammatory autoimmune condition where  usually starts in the sacroiliac joint  which is the joint between your hips and  one of the lower portions of your spine  your sacrum it can get so bad that it  starts spreading to other vertebral  joints moving up your back where you  develop stiffness and in the worst  conditions actually the joints fuse and  you become very stiff it becomes very  painful think about inflammation in the  joint this was about four months ago i  am happy to report that since that time  i've been on medication and i have felt  better than i have felt since like  childhood i take a drug called humera so  no matter where i am no matter what i'm  doing i have to stop what i'm doing and  give myself a little shot  you ready to get poked  we hold on to zack's medicine i have to  take this medication twice a month at  the same time so uh it's 8 40 am and i  got to take this shot this is a  medication that's used as a  disease-modifying anti-rheumatic drug  and these dmards work through different  processes but the medication he takes  targets tumor necrosis factor and it  actually allows the decrease of  inflammation where he has an unnecessary  increase and so what's great is that you  never actually see the needle  oh right that's nice see this window  right here  some yellow liquid is going to pop out  of it  but that's it now i'm done so i started  my medication and for a few months  my life was incredible i was able to  sleep throughout the night without  waking up in pain i wasn't stiff  throughout the day it's important to  remember as he's talking about his  experience with this medication that is  not a cure for his condition it's a  disease modifying drug so it changes the  course the development making the  progression slower that's the most  important takeaway during this time i  was instructed to keep up with physical  activity  but as the months passed i  got a little wax i was back to where i  was the truth is i  started ignoring my disease the second i  could the moment that my medicine  started working i  stopped paying attention to it so it was  time to go back to the doctor finally  ready to accept and confront my symptoms  and ready to attack this sickness head  on and  he said i was fine  my inflammatory markers were still low  if a patient is coming in and saying  they're suffering again with their  illness that they feel like it's making  a return they're going back to where  they were and i come in and i say no  your inflammatory cells are good  that's not adequate medical care we  never treat lab values we don't treat  inflammatory cells if a patient's coming  in and telling me they have back pain if  a patient's telling me that they have  terrible crippling anxiety and  depressive symptoms i don't say oh your  labs look good so deuces i decided to  make my health a full-time job  physical therapy twice a week yoga and  stretching first thing when i wake up  and last thing before i go to bed long  box with bowie every single day instead  of letting someone else walk him for me  and exercise especially on the days that  i don't want to accepting that sometimes  being late to work in the morning is a  necessary component to my health the  movement component and the healing that  comes from moving is amazing because  whether it's a medication a massage  physical therapy all of that is in place  so your body heals itself and a body  that's not in motion will continue  staying not in motion and continue being  in pain and after all that  i feel okay  i still have a baseline of pain  most days i don't know that i'm never  gonna fully be a hundred percent again  but i am done ignoring my pain that's  really powerful because acceptance is a  big part of chronic pain a lot of times  we promise patients that we can fully  get rid of their pain go from 100 to  zero and a lot of times that's not  feasible most notably so when it comes  to opioid treatments patients that are  promised zero pain are giving opioids  their pain goes to zero but then we  can't continue those in many cases long  term unless that there's an end point  there's a goal for something check out  my video of michael reeves and his  bladder exploding what click it it's the  most fascinating story he's actually in  the video with me click here to check  that out and as always stay happy and  healthy
 

